Why it feels like whack-a-mole — and better directions

Shelf-ratio gates fight Goodreads’ messiness: famous books get school/YA/classic noise; sequels don’t. Threshold tweaks always leak somewhere else.

Stronger approaches, in order of leverage:

Series/author priors (we started this) — Treat series as the unit of genre belief; volumes inherit with a confidence prior. Extends cleanly to author-level (“Le Guin Earthsea = fantasy”).

Two-layer model — (a) soft multi-label scores (fantasy 0–1, SF 0–1, …) from shelves, (b) hard filters only for ranking presets. Stop forcing a single boolean when books are genuinely dual (New Sun, Earthsea).

Calibration set — ~200 hand-labeled anchors per gate (positives + hard negatives). Fit weights/thresholds once; refuse changes that fail the set. Turns mole-whacking into regression tests.

Community shelves with reliability weights — Downweight school / read-for-school / generic fiction; trust high-fantasy, space-opera, etc. more.

Optional LLM/embeddings assist — Only for borderline band (e.g. ratio 0.25–0.55), not the whole catalog.

If you want a next step, I’d do (3) calibration fixtures + keep series inheritance — highest accuracy per hour, and it stops silent regressions.



